 To find the greatest possible length in which we can cut the timber into logs with no waste, we need to determine the greatest common divisor (GCD) of the lengths 48, 72, and 40. The GCD of these numbers is the largest number that divides all of them without leaving a remainder. We can use the Euclidean algorithm to find the GCD. First, we find the GCD of 48 and 72, and then we find the GCD of that result and 40. The Euclidean algorithm states that \(\text{GCD}(a, b) = \text{GCD}(b, a \mod b)\). Let's calculate: \[
\begin{align*}
\text{GCD}(48, 72) &= \text{GCD}(72, 48 \mod 72) = \text{GCD}(72, 48) \\
&= \text{GCD}(48, 48 \mod 48) = \text{GCD}(48, 0) = 48.
\end{align*}
\]
Next, we find the GCD of 48 and 40: \[
\begin{align*}
\text{GCD}(48, 40) &= \text{GCD}(40, 48 \mod 40) = \text{GCD}(40, 8) \\
&= \text{GCD}(8, 40 \mod 8) = \text{GCD}(8, 0) = 8.
\end{align*}
\]
Therefore, the greatest possible length in which we can cut the timber into logs with no waste is 8 feet. </think> <answer> 8 </answer>
